.UPDATE...
Issued at 856 AM PDT Tue Oct 20 2026

Looking out the window, yes sometimes we do just look out the
window, we can see cirrus and even some accas. Both of these cloud
types can be precursors to a pattern change. If we take a broader
look at satellite the bands of clouds currently west of Monterey
are the leading edge of an occluded low pressure parked off the
coast. Additionally, the bands east of Monterey are likely tied
to the position of an upper level jet (GOES West shows strong
winds in the 250-450mb range). The accas can be a good indicator
of moisture and lower-mid level instability. If you haven`t
noticed, KMUX is still in precip mode. We are now picking up a few
echoes well offshore closer to the low pressure and surface
boundaries.

No changes in the near term forecast for today. Will be focusing
on the next 24-48 hour as the offshore system moves east bringing
dynamics with it. Early look still suggest some precip across the
entire CWA. The biggest impacts will be tied to the potential
convective threat along the Central Coast. Will coordinate with
SPC to any updates on SVR Outlooks. Still looks like a low chc for
some rotating cells to develop over the coastal waters off Big Sur
Wednesday.

