.SHORT TERM...
Issued at 1203 AM PDT Tue Oct 20 2026
(Today through Wednesday)

The low pressure system is already making its presence known as it
nears our coast. Not only were there various cloud layers present on
Monday, but some measurable rainfall has been observed in
Northern California nearby Eureka (as of 10 PM PDT Monday). Given
that the center of the low is still to our northwest, these
rainbands are not expected to impact us just yet...

Overnight, the upper-level trough continues to dig southward along
the coast and becomes fully cut-off from the main airflow by
Tuesday. During the day, calm conditions are expected with below
normal temperatures and infrequent wind gusts up to 20-25 mph.
Later tonight, the cut-off low begins to slowly propagate
eastward with the first few rainbands possibly impacting the
southern Central Coast first. Through the overnight hours, these
rainbands begin to make their way across the rest of the Central
Coast and Bay Area.

Rain chances increase Wednesday with the North and East Bay less
likely to experience wetting rain (>0.10 inch) during the event.
Chances for wetting rain increase towards the Central Coast where
the center of the cut-off low is expected to enhance moisture
transport onshore. The eastern portion of the system is forecast to
have stronger wind speeds than the western portion, likely due to a
tighter pressure gradient downstream as well as a surge in momentum
flux from the subtropical jet stream. This roughly coincides with
